            "failure_scenario": "The user taps Fact at count 5 (isLoadingFact = true, the effect is running), then taps Reset. The reducer sets count = 0 and fact = nil but doesn't cancel the effect or clear isLoadingFact. The spinner keeps showing, and when .factResponse arrives it sets fact to the fact for 5 while count shows 0. That is the 'reset leaving old state behind' symptom the test name says it catches. The test starts from State(count: 5, fact: \"old\") with isLoadingFact false and no running effect, so it passes against this bug.",
            "evidence": "CounterFeatureTests.swift:36 `TestStore(initialState: CounterFeature.State(count: 5, fact: \"old\"))` never sets isLoadingFact and sends no .factButtonTapped first. CounterFeature.swift:67-70 `case .resetButtonTapped: state.count = 0; state.fact = nil; return .none` leaves isLoadingFact alone and doesn't cancel the effect started at line 49 (`return .run { ... await send(.factResponse(...)) }`, which has no .cancellable id). CounterFeature.swift:58-60 applies any late .factResponse without checking.",
